# Sets, Data and maps

Three types hold several values and are easily confused:

| Type | What it is | Written |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Set<T>` | distinct values of one type, with no positions | `{1, 2, 3}` |
| `Data` | schemaless, heterogeneous, recursively structured runtime data: what JSON or YAML decodes to | `{name: "Alice", age: 42}` |
| `Map<K, V>` | a typed, homogeneous mapping: every key a `K`, every value a `V` | `Map(["a"], [1])` |

`Data` is not a hash map and not a `Map<String, Any>`: its fields may hold
different types, nested as deep as the document goes. A declared structural
type, a record, is a fourth thing again, and is not `Data` or a map merely
because it is written with `{ key: value }` too.

Braces write two of them, and the colon is the discriminator:

> Braces without `:` denote sets. Braces containing key/value entries denote
> `Data`. The empty `Data` literal is `{:}`.

```hql
{}                        // Set<Never>, the empty set
{1, 2, 3}                 // Set<Int>
{"a", "b"}                // Set<String>

{:}                       // Data, the empty record
{name: "Alice"}           // Data
{name: "Alice", age: 42}  // Data
```

A set is values in braces; `Data` is key/value structure in braces. The empty
forms keep exactly that distinction, so no literal takes its meaning from the
type expected of it.

## Empty forms

`{}` is a `Set<Never>`: a set with no elements, of no element type yet. Like
every `Set<Never>`, a declared set type accepts it:

```hql
x : Set<Int> = {}
d : Data = {:}
```

`{:}` is the empty keyed literal. It has no type of its own: it is `Data`
where nothing says otherwise, and the empty map where a map type is declared
for it ([HQL-0032](../../proposals/HQL-0032/README.md)):

| Written | Type | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `{:}` with nothing around it | `Data` | the empty `Data` record, printed `{:}` |
| `d : Data = {:}` | `Data` | the same |
| `m : Map<String, Int> = {:}` | `Map<String, Int>` | an empty map, equal to `Map<String, Int>([], [])` |
| `m : SortedMap<String, Int> = {:}`, or `OrderedMap` | that map type | an empty map of that kind |
| `f({:})` where `f` takes a `Map<String, Int>` | `Map<String, Int>` | an empty map |

An empty literal carries no information, so the type around it decides: HQL
infers a type while the literal says enough, and asks for an explicit one
where it does not. This holds for the empty literal only. A non-empty keyed
literal is always `Data`, so `m : Map<String, Int> = {a: 1}` is refused; a
non-empty map is built with the constructor, `Map(["a"], [1])`.

`d : Data = {}` is a type error, since `{}` is already a set:

```text
type error: `d` is declared Data but its value is Set<Never>
```

The constructor writes the same empty set with its element type,
`Set<Int>([])`, which is how an empty set of a known type prints so that the
text reads back as the same value. The empty `Data` prints as `{:}`, alone or
nested: `{a: {:}}`.

## What each is

A `Set<T>` is a collection of distinct values with no positions, and a
`Map<K, V>` one of unique keys each with a value; see the
[collection model](types/collections/collection-types.md). `Data` is an open
tree whose keys belong to whoever wrote them; see [[data-type]]. A record
literal is not a declared record type: `{name: "Alice"}` is `Data`, and reading
`.name` from it gives an `Option<Data>`.

The rule is specified by [HQL-0029](../../proposals/HQL-0029/README.md), and the
[braces examples](../../../examples/braces/README.md) run it. The book tells
the same story for a first reader in
[sets, Data and maps](../../public/learn/02-data-types/sets-data-and-maps.md).
